{
  "beruf": "kraftfahrer",
  "title": "Driving and delivery",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "french-for-work run, 2026-11-13: written by hand for France, not yet checked by a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Delivering parcels, meals and goods is a common job in French towns, by bike, scooter, van or lorry, often through an agency or an app. This deck covers the vehicle, your daily work, rounds and deliveries, rules and safety, and what you say at the door. Say vous to customers; colleagues at the depot usually say tu. For the driving test itself, see the code de la route vocabulary.",
  "introEasy": "Do you deliver parcels or food, or drive for work in France? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Livreur / livreuse",
    "Chauffeur-livreur / chauffeuse-livreuse",
    "Chauffeur poids lourd",
    "Coursier / coursière",
    "Livreur / livreuse à vélo ou à scooter"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "At the door, one short sentence is enough, always with vous: « Bonjour, j'ai un colis pour madame Martin. Vous pouvez signer ici ? »",
    "At the entryphone, say who you are and what you bring: « Bonjour, c'est le livreur. J'ai un colis pour monsieur Diallo. » If the digicode does not work, call the customer.",
    "At the depot, colleagues and the dispatcher usually say tu: « Tu peux m'aider à décharger ? » With customers, stay with vous.",
    "Driving for work usually needs the right licence category, medical checks and sometimes extra training or papers, and a licence from another country may not be valid for long. Ask your employer, France Travail or a union before you start. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
